we've talked about whether Donald Trump  is a full-blown authoritarian he's not  really a conservative on any issues but  he's also not a liberal it's sort of  hard to pin down what Trump's beliefs  are in some broader sense I want to  present to you a term that explains and  describes the politics of Trump this is  really an important segment okay  regressive populism now hold on a second  I'm not saying regressive as an ad  hominem this has nothing to do with  regressive in the sense that it's  sometimes used as an ad hominem in terms  of regressive left regressive popular  populism is a very specific term and  I've been reading a lot of sort of  academic nonfiction related to politics  and media for a new project I have which  I'll announce at some point soon and I  was reading a book called waves of  rancor by Hilliard and Keith and I came  across a paragraph that described  regressive populism this book is nearly  twenty years old  it describes Donald Trump this paragraph  was distinguishing a different sort of  right-wing movement from the theocratic  right sometimes known as the religious  right there's an alternative called  regressive populism let's take a look at  it because it really explains what Trump  is quote Berlet and Quigley add to this  theocratic right movement others as  advocates of regressive progressive  populism PATRIOT armed militia and white  supremacy groups the overtly racist  far-right organizations such as the Ku  Klux Klan Christian Patriots racist  skinheads neo-nazis and right-wing  revolutionaries promoting in various  combinations and to varying degrees  authoritarianism is in a phobia  conspiracy theories nativism racism  sexism homophobia anti-semitism  demagoguery and scapegoating we've  talked about left-wing vs. right-wing  populism this is much more nuanced and  it's a very relevant perspective and it  really explains what's going on right  now and how it relates to the religious  right which this isn't Trump is not the  religious right Trump talks about God to  pander and he did that in his address to  that joint session of Congress you  today but it's overall a different type  of appeal that Trump is making and it's  sometimes called just populist but most  reasonable people people realize that  it's not the same populism as in the  message of Bernie Sanders was populism  it is regressive populism it's populism  based on conservatism or conserving and  sometimes it's more about not just  conserving but regressing to the past  and that's the opposite of progress all  populism will usually acknowledge  economic and security Bernie did that  Trump did that but regressive populism  proposes as solutions to that inequality  varying degrees of regression this might  be finding scapegoats to blame for  grievances whether it's undocumented  immigrants welfare programs dangerous  Muslims whatever and the solutions  proposed by regressive populism will  further prop up the elites and grow the  kleptocracy or oligarchy progressive  populism the populism of bernie is  actually looking to hold elites in the  wealthy accountable for their role in  creating the problem and make them  responsible for solutions regressive  populism that of Trump actually wants to  deflect attention away from the elites  and the wealthy and big corporations and  scapegoat others usually to benefit the  rich and there's a really big overlap  between the regressive populism of Trump  and the calling cards of the radical  right this includes the neo-nazis the  white supremacists the black separatists  like the Nation of Islam most of those  groups scapegoat a tyrannical government  Jews and minorities very very often  Trump is doing that to varying degrees  and with more subtlety note that none of  this requires Trump or anyone to be  personally racist or anti-semitic if he  has racist or anti-semitic people  involved fine  but I want to be super clear about that  this is an appeal to those ideas and I  think it explains so clearly what we're  seeing right now so I think populism in  a more general sense can often be a good  thing because it means that you're  you're listening to the will of the  constituents and but it has to be kind  of matched with this this sort  of consistency so if you look at Bernie  Sanders he's been the same guy for 30  years in Congress he doesn't really  change based on the political winds he  doesn't change based on political  expediency but Donald Trump I mean he's  he's changed so much in the past ten  years especially and I mean if you look  at this guy he's he's kind of appealed  to the worst in people and that's how he  goes about doing his populism  and I don't think that's the right way  to go you're bringing up something  interesting which is a populist appeal  does not necessarily mean that it is one  that will be good for the majority of  people populism can be appealing to the  masses with particular messages  inclinations or or biases and populism  in and of itself I think is  misunderstood in that it's objectively  progressive and here we are seeing very  specifically regressive populism really  really interesting I recommend the book  waves of rancor from I believe it's from  1999 Hilliard and Keith it really will  break down the different right-wing  movements that exist their use of media  and messaging and I just randomly came  across this paragraph I put it out on my  Instagram page David Pakman and the more  I thought about it the more it clearly  applies to what we're seeing under the  trump administration make sure that you  join us on reddit where you can discuss  this and any other stories that you're  interested in david pakman.com slash  read it that's our e d di t today's show  is brought to you by go daddy where you  can buy your own domain name build a  website or use any of Go Daddy's  business tools you can save 30 percent  at Tri godaddy.com slash David GoDaddy  has actually been the official host of  the David Pakman show